How about a call to the new `push_back`?
```
ms.push_back(37);
BEAST_EXPECT(mutValues[0] == 37);
BEAST_EXPECT(*ms.data() == 1);
```

Micro-optimization:
```
if (b & 0x01)
  return 7;
```
is unnecessary.

Because flags don't get cleared or checked for multiple matches, you could save the overhead of unnecessary `checkEqualForTesting` by moving `if(foundEqual[j]) continue;` to outside the `if`.
```
        for (size_t i = 0; i < subfulfillments_.size(); ++i)
        {
            for (size_t j = 0; j < subfulfillments_.size(); ++j)
            {
                if (foundEqual[j])
                    continue;

                if (c->subfulfillments_[i]->checkEqualForTesting(*subfulfillments_[j]))
                {
                    foundEqual.set(j);
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
```

Same here
```
        for (size_t i = 0; i < subconditions_.size(); ++i)
        {
            for (size_t j = 0; j < subconditions_.size(); ++j)
            {
                if (foundEqual[j])
                    continue;

                if (c->subconditions_[i] == subconditions_[j])
                {
